    "result_text": "The build is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented per-country national-number length validation for E.164 telephone numbers in `OwnerRestControllerV1.java`.\n\n**What changed:**\n\n1. **Added a country-code \u2192 national-length map** (`NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS`): `+61` \u2192 9 national digits, `+1` \u2192 10.\n\n2. **Extended `toE164(...)`** \u2014 after the existing overall \"+\" followed by 8\u201315 digits check, it now also rejects (returns `null`, which surfaces as a 400 via `InvalidOwnerFieldsException`) when the national number has the wrong length for a known country code.\n\n3. **Added `hasValidNationalNumberLength(...)`** \u2014 selects the longest listed country code that prefixes the number, then requires the remaining digits to exactly match that country's requirement. Country codes not in the map carry no per-country requirement (unchanged behaviour), so the existing generic length rule still governs them.\n\n**Why this is consistent with the existing code:**\n- `+61 123` \u2192 national number of 1 digit \u2192 rejected with 400 (the new test).\n- The valid `04XXXXXXXX` mobiles from `uniqueTelephone()` normalize to `+61` + 9 digits \u2192 still accepted.\n- Stored telephones that can't form a valid E.164 number are already *ignored* (not matched) by the duplicate-telephone check, so tightening `toE164` doesn't change duplicate detection semantics.\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
