        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherently placed and well-factored for this codebase:\n\n- **Right unit.** The new `hasValidNationalNumberLength` helper sits as a private sibling to the existing telephone-to-E.164 conversion (and the email normalization helper) already living in the controller. It follows the established placement pattern, so a maintainer would find it natural here rather than surprising.\n- **Factored, not inlined.** Rather than cramming the length check into the conversion method, it's extracted into its own focused method with a single responsibility and clear Javadoc \u2014 the conversion method just adds one guard call, keeping it readable.\n- **No real duplication.** The per-country requirements are centralized in the `NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS` constant and consumed in one place. (The `\"61\"` appearing both in the prepend logic and the map is a pre-existing, mild coupling, not something this diff introduces or worsens.)\n- **Idiomatic.** Uses `Map.of` for the constant and a stream with `max(Comparator.comparingInt(...))` to select the longest-matching country code \u2014 ordinary, readable Java consistent with the surrounding style.\n- **Not a catch-all.** The addition is small and self-contained; it doesn't bloat any method into a grab-bag.\n\nDocumentation was also kept in sync (the conversion method's Javadoc updated to reference the new requirement), which keeps the unit coherent."
